<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.4.3: Exponential Change: The Growth Factor</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01301</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.4.3: Exponential Change: The Growth Factor</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5b419ce8-b5ae-44cc-b192-de2c9889f9f0</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section>
<para>In an exponential function, the output is multiplied by the same factor every time the input increases by one. The multiplier is called the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">growth factor</term>. </para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item> In a biology lab, 500 bacteria reproduce by splitting. Every hour, on the hour, each bacterium splits into two bacteria. Fill in the table by writing an expression to show the number of bacteria after each hour.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="45f1e65a-e06a-4a68-96c1-c4915b0f12af"><problem><para>
      <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
        <thead>
          <row>
            <entry><emphasis>hour</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>number of bacteria</emphasis></entry></row>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <row>
            <entry> 0 </entry>
            <entry> 500 </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 1 </entry>
            <entry>a. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 2 </entry>
            <entry>b. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 3 </entry>
            <entry>c. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> 6 </entry>
            <entry>d. _____</entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry> \(t\) </entry>
            <entry>e. _____</entry>
          </row>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      
      <para>_____ </para>
        </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(500+2\)</para><para>\(500⋅2\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(500⋅2\)</para><para>
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="09d44157-dda4-4f73-a594-d4763128ee00"><problem><para>
      <para> _____</para>
        </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(500+2+2\)</para><para>\(500⋅2⋅2\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(500⋅2⋅2\)</para><para>
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="ccb60b8d-2271-47f4-a71e-5bddc5341d0c"><problem><para>
      <para>_____</para>
        </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(500+2+2+2\)</para><para>\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para><para>
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="f695ce12-a152-4149-a08d-87794c510aa2"><problem><para>
      <para>_____</para>
        </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(500+2+2+2+2\)</para><para>\(500+2+2+2+2+2+2\)</para><para>\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para><para>\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(500⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2⋅2\)</para><para>
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="f73d18f8-613f-407f-ad39-e58f0fb533de"><problem><para>
      <para>_____</para>
        </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(500+2t\)</para><para>\(500+2^t\)</para><para>\(500⋅2^t\)</para><para>\(500⋅2⋅t\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(500⋅2^t\)</para><para>
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section><section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="2857bb7f-d0f9-4dbd-a6cb-ef80fde33db9"><problem><para> 
      <para>Which equation relates \(n\), the number of bacteria, to \(t\), the number of hours?</para>
        </para><para>Multiple Choice:</para><para>\(n=500+2t\)</para><para>\(n=500+2^t\)</para><para>\(n=500⋅2^t\)</para><para>\(n=500⋅2⋅t\)</para></problem><solution><para>\(n=500⋅2^t\)</para><para>
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.4.3. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Use your equation to find \(n\) when \(t\) is 0. What does this value of \(n\) mean in this situation?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> 500; When \(t\) is 0, \(n=500\cdot2^0\). Because \(2^0=1\), \(n=500\cdot1\) or \(n=500\). 500 is the number of bacteria at the starting time or at hour 0.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> In a different biology lab, a population of single-cell parasites also reproduces hourly. An equation that gives the number of parasites, \(p\), after \(t\) hours is \(p=100\cdot3^t\). Explain what the numbers 100 and 3 mean in this situation. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> 100 is the number of parasites at hour 0 because \(100\cdot3^0=100\). The number 3 means that every hour, the number of parasites triples.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
